(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-benign-skin-lesions-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Benign Skin Lesions Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Why does a maturing scar gain tensile strength?","choices":["Progressive accumulation of keratin within the dermis","Progressive deposition of lipid-laden macrophages","Progressive cross-linking of collagen fibers","Progressive proliferation of endothelial cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the remodeling phase is where that happens. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What are the first-line treatments for an infantile hemangioma?","choices":["Cryotherapy with liquid nitrogen, repeated until the lesion has flattened completely","Beta-blockers and corticosteroids; both are named first line","Intralesional corticosteroid alone, with fluorouracil added if the lesion continues to enlarge","Surgical excision in every case, performed before the proliferative phase has finished"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — propranolol by mouth or timolol topically, whose mechanism is not well understood, and corticosteroids given topically, intralesionally or orally to slow growth and shrink the lesion. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What should the history cover in a patient with spider angiomas?","choices":["Footwear, occupation and use of tools or sports equipment","Immobility, incontinence and nutritional status","Pregnancies, hormone use, alcohol history, and medications that carry a risk of liver damage","Birth history, feeding and weight gain, and any family history of vascular birthmarks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the lesion can be a marker of estrogen excess or liver disease. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"How does nevus flammeus behave over a lifetime?","choices":["Present at birth, grows with the child, never involutes, and becomes darker and thicker","Present at birth, proliferates for months, then involutes completely by school age","Present at birth, fades within a year or persists only on the neck","Appears with age, remains under five millimetres, and new lesions continue to develop"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the psychosocial burden of facial disfigurement can be overwhelming. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"How is pilonidal disease managed at each stage?","choices":["Keep the area clean and free of debris, with shaving or laser hair therapy; an acute abscess needs incision and drainage; chronic disease may need surgical referral for excision","Keep the area covered and moist with hydrocolloid dressings; an acute abscess is treated with oral antibiotics alone; chronic disease resolves without intervention","Intralesional corticosteroid for the acute lesion, followed by silicone sheeting and compression for the chronic phase","Observation only, since most lesions involute spontaneously by school age"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — hygiene first, drainage for the abscess, surgery for the chronic tract. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What is the differential diagnosis for sebaceous hyperplasia, and how is it settled?","choices":["Squamous cell carcinoma; excisional biopsy is the only reliable way to distinguish them","Cutaneous melanoma; the dimple sign distinguishes the two on examination","Pyogenic granuloma; the epithelial collarette distinguishes the two","Basal cell skin cancer; dermoscopy can distinguish the two, with biopsy if the concern remains"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — and the patient often presents worried about exactly that. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"What is the differential diagnosis for a lipoma?","choices":["Milia, xanthelasma and basal cell carcinoma","Nevus flammeus and pyogenic granuloma","Epidermal cyst, dermatofibroma and abscess","Squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma and amelanotic melanoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — the diagnosis is typically clinical. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"What is the sex ratio for pilonidal cyst, and what was the old belief about its origin?","choices":["Male to female three to one; originally thought congenital, now believed acquired","Female to male three to one; originally thought acquired, now believed congenital","Male to female two to one; thought to arise from the pilosebaceous unit","Female to male two to one; thought to follow trauma or an insect bite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and recurrence is common. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What is the triphasic pattern of a keratoacanthoma?","choices":["Rapid growth within six to eight weeks, then stabilization, then regression after three to six months","Rapid proliferation for four to six months, then slowing between six and twelve months, then involution over years","Slow development over months to years, with continued enlargement and no regression at all","Appearance within four weeks of the injury, a period of stability, then flattening with time"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — although it may continue growing or, rarely, metastasize. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which pressure reproduces the pain of a wart?","choices":["Side pressure","Direct downward pressure","Deep palpation of the surrounding tissue","Lateral compression producing a dimple"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a corn hurts with direct downward pressure instead. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What opportunity does the deck say a new lesion in a sun-exposed area creates?","choices":["To screen for hyperlipidemia and assess cardiac risk","To refer to podiatry if the patient is diabetic","To advise avoiding cosmetic procedures such as ear piercing","To counsel on sunscreen, peak-hour sun avoidance and skin checks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the general education point the deck attaches to every benign lesion in a sun-exposed area. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"How is nevus flammeus managed?","choices":["Observation alone, because the lesion fades spontaneously during the first year of life","No treatment; tinted waterproof makeup, or pulsed dye laser","Oral propranolol or topical timolol as first line, with corticosteroids as the alternative","Surgical excision in infancy, before the lesion has had time to thicken and darken"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the laser selectively destroys superficial target vessels by inducing intravascular coagulation, which is later absorbed and replaced by collagen. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What is the sex ratio for dermatofibroma, and what may precede it?","choices":["Female to male three to one, in Caucasians; it appears in the first weeks of life","Male to female one to two, in all races; it may follow trauma, viral infection or an insect bite","Male to female two to one, in all races; it arises from the pilosebaceous unit after sun exposure","Male to female three to one, in Caucasians; it follows disruption of the skin over the coccyx"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the etiology is otherwise uncertain. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"How does a hypertrophic scar differ from a keloid on four counts?","choices":["It arises from endothelial proliferation, appears in the first weeks of life, grows for months, and involutes over years","It arises from dermal fibroblast clusters, follows trauma or an insect bite, stays under a centimetre, and shows a dimple sign","It arises in the active proliferative phase, develops within four weeks, stays confined to the wound margins, and regresses with time","It arises from an unclear fibroproliferative process, develops over months to years, extends beyond the wound margins, and does not regress"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — every one of those four inverts the keloid. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How are corns and calluses managed?","choices":["Elliptical surgical excision with five-millimetre margins, sent for histology","Padding and avoiding poorly fitting footwear, with over-the-counter keratolytic products","Intralesional corticosteroid injection, with silicone gel sheeting worn daily","Cryotherapy with liquid nitrogen, repeated at two-week intervals until resolved"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — remove the pressure first, then thin the keratin. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What post-surgical advice is given to a patient prone to keloids?","choices":["Wear properly fitting shoes and socks and use padding inside the shoe","Avoid stretching the immature scar, avoid hot baths, keep the wound clean, and avoid body piercings","Massage the scar firmly twice daily, take warm baths, and expose the site to sunlight","Reposition every two hours, manage incontinence, and apply barrier creams"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — hot baths can aggravate surgery-induced inflammation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the differential diagnosis for a dermatofibroma?","choices":["Squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma, amelanotic melanoma and molluscum contagiosum","Cystic acne, lipoma, neurofibroma, keratoacanthoma and basal cell carcinoma","Milia, xanthelasma and basal cell carcinoma, all of which sit around the eye","Basal cell carcinoma, hypertrophic scar, cutaneous melanoma and keratoacanthoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — two of the four are malignancies, which is why it matters. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is intralesional methotrexate used for in keratoacanthoma, and how does it work?","choices":["Given before excision to reduce the size of the lesion; it inhibits deoxyribonucleic acid synthesis in actively dividing cells","Given after excision in order to prevent recurrence; it inhibits fibroblast proliferation within the healing wound bed","Given instead of excision in frail patients; it induces microvascular thrombosis within the vessels feeding the lesion","Given topically over several months; it slows the turnover of sebocytes within the enlarged sebaceous gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a chemotherapeutic agent used as a surgical adjunct. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What defines a stage 2 pressure injury?","choices":["Full thickness skin loss in which adipose tissue is clearly visible in the wound bed, but fascia and muscle are not","A localised area of non-blanchable erythema over completely intact skin, with no loss of epidermis anywhere in it","Obscured full thickness skin and tissue loss whose depth cannot be judged because slough or eschar covers the bed","Partial-thickness skin loss with exposed dermis; the wound bed is viable and pink or red, and may be moist, shiny or dry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — viable dermis, and no fat visible. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What should be screened for in a patient with xanthelasma, and why does it matter?","choices":["Diabetes mellitus; it may signify increased risk of peripheral neuropathy","Thyroid dysfunction; it may signify increased risk of periorbital myxoedema","Liver disease; it may signify increased risk of variceal bleeding","Hyperlipidemia; it may signify increased risk of cardiac disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the lesion is associated with lipid disorders. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What is the differential diagnosis given for a keloid?","choices":["Hypertrophic scar, dermatofibroma, foreign-body granuloma","Squamous cell carcinoma, basal cell carcinoma, amelanotic melanoma","Cystic acne, lipoma, neurofibroma, basal cell carcinoma","Cherry angioma, malignant melanoma, squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the same three are listed for hypertrophic scar. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What do syringomas look like, and where do they sit?","choices":["Multiple whitish-yellow papules of two to nine millimetres with central umbilication, most often on the face","Soft yellow cholesterol plaques, most often on the medial eyelids","Light tan to brown macules over five millimetres, appearing in the first year of life","Multiple skin-coloured, pink or brown papules of one to two millimetres, most often on the eyelids and upper cheeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the periorbital location is the recognisable part. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which lesions can a cutaneous horn arise from?","choices":["Cherry angioma, spider angioma, pyogenic granuloma and telangiectasia, all of which are vascular","Epidermoid cyst, lipoma, syringoma and sebaceous hyperplasia, all of which are adnexal or soft tissue","Actinic keratosis, warts, seborrheic keratosis, keratoacanthoma, and basal or squamous cell carcinoma","Keloid, hypertrophic scar, dermatofibroma and foreign-body granuloma, all of which are fibrous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — benign and malignant bases both occur, which is the whole problem. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is inside an epidermoid cyst, and why is the common name wrong?","choices":["Keratin, from epithelium enclosed within the dermis; it is not a sebaceous cyst","Sebum, from a sebaceous gland whose duct has become obstructed; the common name sebaceous cyst is therefore accurate","Mucin, extruded from an adjacent joint space into the surrounding dermis, where it compacts the cells at its margin","Lipid-laden macrophages, collecting as soft yellow plaques beneath the thin skin of the medial eyelid"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — it is called a sebaceous cyst because the contents look like sebum, but the substance is keratin and the deck states outright that it is not one. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What size defines a café au lait spot before and after puberty, and how many are diagnostic?","choices":["Over five millimetres at any age; the number is not part of any diagnostic criterion","Over five millimetres prepubertal and over fifteen millimetres postpubertal; six or more are diagnostic","Over fifteen millimetres prepubertal and over five millimetres postpubertal; three or more are diagnostic","Over one millimetre at any age; two or more are diagnostic in a child under five"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — though the macules alone do not establish the diagnosis. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What should a patient never do to a skin tag at home?","choices":["Cover it with an occlusive dressing, because it will become infected","Expose it to sunlight, because it will darken permanently","Cut it or pull it off, because it can bleed when it comes away","Apply an over-the-counter keratolytic to it, because it will scar"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and a new tag often forms in the same area anyway. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"How is syringoma managed, and what is the trade-off?","choices":["For cosmesis only: intralesional corticosteroid injection flattens the lesions reliably, with local tissue atrophy as the only meaningful risk","Always excised surgically, because the lesions cannot be reliably distinguished from a basal cell carcinoma on clinical grounds","Always simply observed, because the lesions resolve spontaneously within about a year of first appearing at puberty","For cosmesis only: drugs risk recurrence, and removal procedures risk a poor cosmetic result"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — every option trades one against the other. The drug named is oral isotretinoin; the procedures are curettage and electrodesiccation, laser therapy, cryotherapy and surgical excision. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How does a keloid feel and what symptoms does it cause?","choices":["Translucent skin-coloured papules over the distal interphalangeal joint; asymptomatic unless they grow large","Firm bulbous nodules or markedly elevated plaques; usually asymptomatic, but may be pruritic or burning","Soft rubbery subcutaneous nodules under five centimetres; painless unless adjoining structures are invaded","Soft skin-coloured pedunculated papules on a thin stalk; asymptomatic unless caught and traumatised"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — patients also present over cosmetic concern alone. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What should be done with an inflamed epidermoid cyst?","choices":["Incise and drain it, then pack the cavity and allow the wound to close slowly by secondary intention","Leave it entirely alone, on the basis that an inflamed cyst always settles without any intervention at all","Postpone excision for a few weeks, reduce the inflammation with intralesional triamcinolone, and give antibiotics if needed","Excise it immediately, on the basis that inflammation indicates the capsule has already ruptured into the surrounding dermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — surgery is done when the cyst is not inflamed. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How is a dermatofibroma managed?","choices":["Cryotherapy with liquid nitrogen repeated at monthly intervals until the hyperpigmented halo has resolved completely","Often no treatment at all; a small lesion takes a shave or punch biopsy, a larger one may need excision","Immediate wide local excision in every case, because a dermatofibroma cannot reliably be told from a cutaneous melanoma on clinical appearance alone","Intralesional corticosteroid injection followed by silicone gel sheeting, on the basis that the lesion is a form of abnormal scarring"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — treat only if the diagnosis is questioned or symptoms warrant it. In a small lesion the biopsy is both diagnostic and therapeutic, doing two jobs at once. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 47"}]};
